      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "A 63-year-old woman had chest pain that worsened when nifedipine was given. She was found to have a right coronary-to-thorax angioma/fistula. Acute nifedipine administration elicited precordial symptoms, associated with ischemic changes in the inferior ECG leads and an obvious vasodilatation of the fistulous tract, suggesting that a nifedipine-induced \"steal\" phenomenon might be responsible for the paradoxic clinical response."},
